  "id": 203986872007433,
  "uuid": "f147bad3-8f55-4699-85d8-74c02b3e47fe",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "cff9e1c9-33a8-6027-7b8a-4e2c1c2211eb",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "5db60d37-b32e-1329-9018-2f8b0edad979",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The hybrid session allowed both in-person and virtual attendees to engage actively. She critically examined the prevalent classification of English-medium private schools in Pakistan, challenging the simplistic elite/non-elite and rich/poor dichotomies. She highlighted the existence of middle-tier English-medium private schools that do not fit neatly into these binary categories.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr Pervez provided a nuanced view of these middle-tier schools, noting that the families of students in these institutions primarily speak Urdu or regional languages at home, with parents typically holding tertiary degrees and middle management positions. This contrasts with parents of low-fee school students, who often lack education and work in manual labor roles.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Her presentation also placed middle-tier schools within the global educational discourse on language immersion and submersion, exploring whether the language learning experiences of Pakistani students in these schools are additive or subtractive. Drawing from her personal experiences as an educator and parent, as well as empirical findings from a case study at a middle-tier school in Karachi, Dr Pervez emphasized the need to move beyond simplistic binaries. She advocated for a deeper exploration of how bilingualism/multilingualism impacts a wide range of Pakistani students, not just those from lower socioeconomic backgrounds.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The seminar concluded with a lively Q&A session, reflecting the audience's keen interest in the topic. The presentation provided valuable insights into middle-tier schools, urging educators and researchers to adopt a more nuanced approach in understanding and addressing the educational needs of Pakistani students.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
